{"conjecture": "Long-running systems degrade not through individual errors but through the systematic destruction of counterfactual alternatives: each locally correct change prunes the space of nearby designs the system could tolerate, so the system becomes a narrow ridge rather than a broad plateau. Reviewers and tests validate the chosen path but are blind to the unchosen paths that were silently eliminated, making future changes harder because the safe neighborhood has shrunk even though no rule was broken. If this is correct, then the key metric of architectural health is not defect rate but the measured width of the design-tolerance neighborhood, and systems should be tested against random perturbations of their structure, not just their behavior."}
